In all the algorithms below, the symplectic Euler scheme can be taken place by the St"{o}rmer-Verlet scheme, i.e.  egin{equation} \left\{ egin{aligned} & v_{k+1/2} = v_{k} -  rac{h}{2}
abla f(x_{k}) \ & x_{k+1} = x_{k} + h v_{k + 1/2} \ & v_{k+1} = v_{k + 1/2} -  rac{h}{2} 
abla f(x_{k+1}) \end{aligned}ight. \end{equation} which works perfectly better than the symplectic scheme even if doubling step size and keep the left-right symmetry of the Hamiltonian system. The St"{o}rmer-Verlet scheme is the natural discretization for 2nd-order ODE  egin{equation} x_{k+1} - 2x_{k} + x_{k-1} = -h^{2}
abla f(x_{k}) \end{equation} which is named as leap-frog scheme in PDEs. We remark that the discrete scheme~(ef{eqn:discrete}) is different from the finite difference approximation by the forward Euler method to analyze the stability of 2nd ODE in~\citep{su2014differential}, since the momentum term is biased.
